[cot:thought] The em dash in the source file is encoded as UTF-8 byte sequence E2-80-94. PowerShell's Select-String cmdlet renders it as a hyphen rather than the actual dash character.
[cot:thought] QA was searching for
[cot:thought] an ASCII hyphen instead of the existing em dash. Playwright waitFor may treat sr-only elements as visible, since the smoke test passed despite using the same selector.
[cot:thought] Playwright's default
[cot:thought] waitFor state is visible. sr-only elements are clipped and absolutely positioned, so they likely fail visibility checks. The smoke test passed because getByText matched the marker, possibly because the element is not fully hidden.
[cot:thought] Tailwind's `sr-only`
[cot:thought] class applies `clip: rect(0, 0, 0, 0)` and `overflow: hidden`, which likely empties the element's bounding box and causes Playwright to treat it as invisible.
[cot:thought] This contradicts the smoke test passing, since a failed `waitFor` would have thrown a timeout error. The smoke test may have used a different assertion, or the built output may have contained older visible text.
[cot:thought] The smoke test likely
[cot:thought] passed because the marker text also appears in visible content. The QA script will be updated to search for the em dash character directly and use `state: 'attached'` instead of `visible`.
