[cot:thought] The core narrative
[cot:thought] compares funding levels across public-safety tiers. Teachers score 76.4% weighted funded.
[cot:thought] Local police and fire are 76.7%. Statewide public safety is 82.1%. State employee plans are 65.7%. The data shows local police and fire are not systematically underfunded relative to teachers.
[cot:thought] Local police and fire
[cot:thought] plans outperform state-employee-only plans at 65.7%. The median-funded-ratio headline requires plan-level medians, which will be approximated from Equable rankings and CAFR disclosures rather than relying solely on published averages.
[cot:thought] Specific local police
[cot:thought] and fire plans reveal wide variation. Detroit P&F Plan 1 leads at 109.1%. Houston Firefighters and LA Fire and Police follow at 98.9% and 98.6%. Jacksonville P&F and Dallas P&F trail at 43.1% and 40.6%.
[cot:thought] Chicago Police and Firefighters are at 31.1% and 21.6%. Utah Firefighters lead statewide public safety at 111.9%.
[cot:thought] Washington Public Safety Plan 2 and Arizona Public Safety Tier 3 are near 108%. DC Police & Fire and Utah Public Safety are at 104%.
[cot:thought] NJ Police & Fire State Division is at 48.2%. Teachers frequently appear in the bottom ten.
[cot:thought] The headline will
[cot:thought] anchor on a median funded-ratio gap. Local police and fire plans trail teachers by roughly 0.3 percentage points. Statewide public safety trails by 5.7 points. The narrative will contrast these narrow averages against median gaps and left-tail distress.
[cot:thought] I will assemble a
[cot:thought] balanced sample of large plans with disclosed funded ratios and label it as a desk estimate drawn from Equable, CRR, and CAFR disclosures.
[cot:thought] I am also fetching additional plan-level data from the Equable SOP 2025 text and checking whether a 2026 version contains a FY2025-type table. To satisfy the chart diversity requirement without cloning the pension dashboard, I will source a second recent visualization pattern.
