[cot:thought] Retrieving the current PDF and waiting for FRED downloads to finish before parsing the data.
[cot:thought] Checking for a Census
[cot:thought] Bureau demo key. If unavailable, I'll extract figures directly from the PDF and published tables. Other posts in this repo typically hand-compile disclosed numbers with confidence labels. The national HBA conversion rate is roughly 27% within eight quarters, though state variation exists.
[cot:thought] Application counts per employer will be calculated by dividing HBA totals by the eight-quarter cohort size.
[cot:thought] Clarified the conversion
[cot:thought] metric: BF8Q represents business formations occurring within eight quarters of each application cohort, divided by total high-propensity applications. This aligns with Census methodology.
[cot:thought] BF series cover all
[cot:thought] business applications rather than high-propensity ones alone. The 27 percent conversion rate applies specifically to HBA. The requested three-year window exceeds the available eight-quarter public series, so BDS employer firm births may serve as a proxy denominator.
[cot:thought] The ratio HBA divided
[cot:thought] by SBF8Q yields applications per eventual employer formation from the same vintage. Because BF8Q captures all business applications, its share of HBA can exceed the theoretical HBA conversion rate; published transition rates are preferable.
[cot:thought] Nationally, roughly 27 percent of HBA become employers, compared to about 3.8 percent for NHBA. Aggregate BF8Q over total BA is lower because HBA historically represents only 30 to 40 percent of all applications.
[cot:thought] Confirmed the headline metric as HBA divided by BF8Q. State-level data will be assembled from the PDF, FRED state series, and published Census tables. Awaiting the FRED download and checking for a Census API key.
