[cot:thought] Series naming is finalized. Seasonally adjusted BAHBATOTALSAxx data will serve as the HBA metric.
[cot:thought] BF8Q spliced series
[cot:thought] will use BFSBF8QTOTALNSAxx. All 50 states plus DC will be downloaded for both metrics. Conversion rates and applications per employer will be computed for cohorts since 2020. BF8Q spans eight quarters, roughly two years, so it will be used with a caveat.
[cot:thought] The method sums HBA from January 2020 through December 2022 and BF8Q for matching application months.
[cot:thought] The BF8Q-to-HBA ratio
[cot:thought] overstates true HBA conversion because BF8Q includes non-high-propensity employer formations.
[cot:thought] The revised approach uses the published national HBA transition rate as an anchor.
[cot:thought] State-level analysis will rely on overall BF8Q-to-BA conversion and HBA-to-BA propensity share.
[cot:thought] The headline will
[cot:thought] frame the story as "Applications per new employer," calculated as HBA divided by BF8Q. This provides a direct count of how many applications spawn each new employer.
[cot:thought] The conversion share remains a secondary desk proxy. Census BF series track which applications become employers.
[cot:thought] High HBA-to-BF8Q ratios
[cot:thought] may reflect loose propensity labeling or unusually high application volumes relative to eventual payroll births. A Node script will fetch all states and build the data module.
